{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-THERMAL", "keywords": "thermal temperature hot heat Arm CPU GPU clock frequency throttling slow performance", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-3bplus", "raspberry-pi-4", "raspberry-pi-5"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Frequency management and thermal control", "sha256": "c2a9141b012abdb2bdd53627e80a72e2077889abcd1d2bba0cf5d2cc42c43e7c", "status": "current", "text": "Between 80 and 85 degrees Celsius, Raspberry Pi Arm cores progressively throttle. At 85 degrees Celsius, both Arm and GPU throttle. This describes thermal behavior, not a completed repair.", "title": "Thermal throttling", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/frequency-management.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-PI5-FAN", "keywords": "fan off spin start warm warming temperature default threshold speed", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-5"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Raspberry Pi 5 fans", "sha256": "c2a9141b012abdb2bdd53627e80a72e2077889abcd1d2bba0cf5d2cc42c43e7c", "status": "current", "text": "Raspberry Pi 5 defaults keep the fan off below 50 degrees Celsius during warming. Speeds at 50, 60, 67.5, and 75 degrees Celsius are respectively 30%, 50%, 70%, and 100%. Configuration can change these defaults.", "title": "Raspberry Pi 5 fan thresholds", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/frequency-management.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-PI5-HYST", "keywords": "fan cooling falling temperature cool down hysteresis threshold remains running", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-5"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Raspberry Pi 5 fans", "sha256": "c2a9141b012abdb2bdd53627e80a72e2077889abcd1d2bba0cf5d2cc42c43e7c", "status": "current", "text": "As Raspberry Pi 5 cools, fan-speed reductions occur 5 degrees Celsius below the warming thresholds. This hysteresis and the speed thresholds are configurable.", "title": "Raspberry Pi 5 fan hysteresis", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/frequency-management.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-TEMPERATURE", "keywords": "temperature measurement sensor Linux reading vcgencmd measure_temp GPU", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-3bplus", "raspberry-pi-4", "raspberry-pi-5"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Measure temperatures", "sha256": "c2a9141b012abdb2bdd53627e80a72e2077889abcd1d2bba0cf5d2cc42c43e7c", "status": "current", "text": "The documented vcgencmd measure_temp command reads the current SoC temperature through the GPU. Linux temperature readings can differ because of the monitoring implementation.", "title": "Reading SoC temperature", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/frequency-management.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-PI3-SOFT", "keywords": "thermal temperature soft limit clock frequency Arm CPU 60 throttling", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-3bplus"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Frequency management and thermal control", "sha256": "c2a9141b012abdb2bdd53627e80a72e2077889abcd1d2bba0cf5d2cc42c43e7c", "status": "current", "text": "Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+ has a configurable 60-degree-Celsius soft limit. Reaching it reduces the clock from 1.4 to 1.2 GHz; the hard limit remains 85 degrees Celsius.", "title": "Raspberry Pi 3 Model B+ soft limit", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/frequency-management.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-PI4-POWER", "keywords": "power supply voltage current USB C adapter connector 3A", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-4"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Recommended power supplies", "sha256": "49f6478c1c0164237ebcf1d28aac4709e843d2b5c06442539bacae05e49eafb1", "status": "current", "text": "Raspberry Pi 4 uses USB-C power. The documentation specifies a 5.1 V supply and recommends the 3 A USB-C supply for this model.", "title": "Raspberry Pi 4 power supply", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/power-supplies.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-PI5-USB", "keywords": "USB peripheral current limit budget disk power supply 5A 3A 600mA 1.6A", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-5"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Typical power requirements", "sha256": "49f6478c1c0164237ebcf1d28aac4709e843d2b5c06442539bacae05e49eafb1", "status": "current", "text": "With a supply capable of 5 A at 5 V, Raspberry Pi 5 provides 1.6 A to downstream USB peripherals. Other compatible supplies restrict that allowance to 600 mA. Connected peripherals share the available budget.", "title": "Raspberry Pi 5 USB current budget", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/power-supplies.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-LOW-VOLTAGE", "keywords": "low voltage undervoltage warning kernel log power supply cable USB brownout", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-3bplus", "raspberry-pi-4", "raspberry-pi-5"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Power supply warnings", "sha256": "49f6478c1c0164237ebcf1d28aac4709e843d2b5c06442539bacae05e49eafb1", "status": "current", "text": "The documented low-voltage detection threshold is 4.63 V with a 5% tolerance; detection creates a kernel-log entry. Peripheral demand, an inadequate supply, or cable resistance can cause voltage drops. A warning alone does not identify which cause applies.", "title": "Low-voltage warnings", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/power-supplies.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
{"attribution": "Raspberry Pi Ltd; passages summarized by the project assistant.", "id": "RP-BACKPOWER", "keywords": "USB hub back power backpower upstream powered protection surge", "license": "CC-BY-SA-4.0", "models": ["raspberry-pi-3bplus", "raspberry-pi-4", "raspberry-pi-5"], "owner_id": null, "revision": null, "section": "Back-powering", "sha256": "49f6478c1c0164237ebcf1d28aac4709e843d2b5c06442539bacae05e49eafb1", "status": "current", "text": "A USB device supplying current upstream can back-power the Raspberry Pi. The documentation discourages this because power delivered through the hub bypasses the board protection circuitry.", "title": "USB back-powering", "url": "https://github.com/raspberrypi/documentation/blob/9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f/documentation/asciidoc/computers/raspberry-pi/power-supplies.adoc", "version": "9bb5ef62d9d5e32930d5d79ae2e05cf75f0eea4f"}
